1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. c3 { This is the Ponziani Opening, one of the oldest chess openings. White just wants to build a strong centre with d4, but of course we will try to stop it. -KEY- } d5 { A logical continuation fighting for the centre. As we will see, some of the lines are quite sharp and forcing but these usually are objectively very good for us. } 4. Bb5 $2 { Surprisingly enough, this bad move is White's second most popular try. Black should be more than happy to see it appear on the board. } dxe4 5. Nxe5 Qg5 $1 { The kingside attack is more important than White's pin. } 6. d4 Qxg2 7. Rf1 Bd6 $1 { It's important to know White's not winning a piece here. } 8. Nxc6 Bd7 9. Nxa7 c6 $19 { Black not only regains a piece but White's position is in ruins. } 
