1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. c3 { This is the Ponziani Opening, one of the oldest chess openings. White just wants to build a strong centre with d4, but of course we will try to stop it. } d5 { A logical continuation fighting for the centre. As we will see, some of the lines are quite sharp and forcing but these usually are objectively very good for us. } 4. Bb5 $2 { Surprisingly enough, this bad move is White's second most popular try. Black should be more than happy to see it appear on the board. } dxe4 5. Nxe5 Qg5 $1 { The kingside attack is more important than White's pin. } 6. Qa4 { -KEY- } Qxg2 7. Bxc6+ bxc6 8. Qxc6+ Kd8 9. Rf1 { All forced and Black is winning but he has to allow White to capture on a8 with a check. } Bh3 $1 { Once we take on f1 White's position is collapsing. } 10. Qxa8+ Ke7 { There is no way to protect f1. } 11. Kd1 ( 11. Nc6 { can be met by } Kd6 { or just } Kf6 12. Qd8 Kg6 $1 { when White doesn't have good checks and the rook on f1 falls } ) 
Qxf1+ 12. Kc2 Nh6 $19 { Preparing ...f6 or ...Kf6. Both moves wouldn't be good right away because of b3 and Qxf8 respectively. Black is winning. The critical variation runs as follows: } 13. Qc6 f6 14. Qxc7+ Ke6 15. d4 fxe5 16. Qxe5+ Kf7 17. Bxh6 Qd3+ 18. Kc1 gxh6 19. Qh5+ Kf6 20. Qe5+ Kg6 21. Qxh8 Qf1+ 22. Kc2 Qxf2+ 23. Nd2 e3 24. Rg1+ Kh5 25. Qe5+ Bf5+ 26. Kb3 exd2 27. Rd1 Kg6 $19 
