1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. c3 { This is the Ponziani Opening, one of the oldest chess openings. White just wants to build a strong centre with d4, but of course we will try to stop it. } d5 { A logical continuation fighting for the centre. As we will see, some of the lines are quite sharp and forcing but these usually are objectively very good for us. } 4. exd5 $6 { -KEY- } Qxd5 { Like in the Sicilian Alapin, White can't gain a tempo with Nc3. } 5. d4 Nf6 { Black enjoys free development and only White can be worried about his position. } 6. dxe5 $6 Qxd1+ 7. Kxd1 Ng4 $17 { This is obviously better for Black. } 
