1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. c3 { This is the Ponziani Opening, one of the oldest chess openings. White just wants to build a strong centre with d4, but of course we will try to stop it. } d5 { A logical continuation fighting for the centre. As we will see, some of the lines are quite sharp and forcing but these usually are objectively very good for us. } 4. Qa4 { The main move. -KEY- } f6 { That looks ugly but there was no convenient way to protect e5. } 5. d3 { This passive move gives Black a free hand to comfortably deploy his forces. However it's not that bad and can lead to original play with chances for both sides. } Be6 6. Be2 Ne7 7. O-O a6 { A nice little flexible move that discourages Nbd2-b3 as we have ...b5. Black will continue with ...Qd7 (or ...Qd6 in some cases!), ...0-0-0 and also pushing pawns on the starting with ...g5 is an idea. This position has been tested in correspondence play and the conclusion is clear: White has to be more accurate to maintain equality. Black player does not need to know much, as the position is very easy to play. } 8. Be3 $6 Qd7 9. Nbd2 O-O-O 10. Nb3 { Threatening Nc5 but we are just in time to face this. } Nb8 11. Qxd7+ Nxd7 $15 { The endgame is slightly better for Black thanks to the space advantage. } 
 *

